* Fri Oct 02 2026 nicolataibi <nicola.taibi.1967@gmail.com> - 2026.10.02.04
  - Server: added a professional non-blocking network I/O mode, selectable
    at startup with --io=async; the default --io=legacy keeps the current
    bounded behavior (blocking read_all() with the 5 s poll in the
    single-threaded epoll loop, src/server/net.c) unchanged. In legacy
    mode a client that sends a header and then goes silent holds the whole
    loop for up to 5 seconds, freezing every other player - an app-level
    DoS in a 60 Hz real-time engine. The async mode implements the proper
    architecture: (1) accepted client sockets are explicitly non-blocking
    (fcntl O_NONBLOCK); (2) the request handler never waits on a socket -
    no while/poll cycle inside it; (3) a recv() returning
    EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK mid-packet stages the bytes already read into the
    per-connection persistent RX buffer and returns to the epoll loop
    immediately; when EPOLLIN fires again the state machine
    (CONN_S_HEADER -> CONN_S_BODY -> CONN_S_TEXT) resumes from the saved
    cursor and dispatches the packet only once it is complete, so a
    stalled or malicious client can at most occupy its own session, never
    the loop. New files: src/server/conn.c + include/conn.h (session pool,
    MAX_CONNS = 4*MAX_CLIENTS, which is also a DoS guard against unbounded
    fd growth; the RX state machine; small-reply TX staging drained on
    EPOLLOUT so the handler never blocks on send either; reap_player_fd()
    shared slot reclamation; run_epoll_loop_async()) and
    src/server/packets.c + include/packets.h (the packet dispatcher,
    extracted verbatim from the inline loop and shared by both modes via
    the PktIO body source: legacy reads bodies from the socket with
    read_all(), async consumes the staged buffer). The shared dispatcher
    routes system messages (send_server_msg) to the thread pool in async
    mode, so the loop and the 60 Hz game thread never block on writes,
    and write_all() gained an EAGAIN guard (bounded POLLOUT wait, 30 s
    total timeout) required for non-blocking sockets and inert on the
    legacy blocking ones. Micro-fixes in the shared path (both modes):
    pkt.cmd is now NUL-terminated before process_command (the pattern
    already used for pkt.name - a 256-byte command could overrun the
    strlen); the unsafe-name reject no longer breaks out of the event
    batch (level-triggered epoll re-reports dropped events anyway); slot
    reclamation now happens before close(fd) (the order was previously
    mixed, leaving a re-association window). The pre-existing slot leak
    on unknown-type close (players[i].socket left set on a closed fd,
    self-healing at the next login of the same name) is intentionally
    unchanged in both modes. Verified: clean rebuild of all targets (C23,
    -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic, no new warnings) and existing unit suite
    7/7. Smoke test: pending (run by the user, both --io modes).

