* Thu Oct 01 2026 nicolataibi <nicola.taibi.1967@gmail.com> - 2026.10.01.05
  - Vulkan client: fixed a latent out-of-bounds read of the shared-state
    object array in mainLoop() (src/spacegl_vulkan.c, audit item B8:
    loop bound MAX_NET_OBJECTS on a MAX_OBJECTS array). The "Detect New
    Server Frame" block walks the IPC frame handed over by the client
    (st = shm->buffers[read_index], GameState) with
    for (o = 0; o < st->object_count && o < MAX_NET_OBJECTS; o++) and
    indexes st->objects[o] on every iteration — but st->objects is
    SharedObject[MAX_OBJECTS] (256 slots, shared_state.h) while
    MAX_NET_OBJECTS is 1024 (game_state.h): had object_count carried a
    value in (256, 1024], the loop would read up to 768 SharedObject
    slots past the end of the frame's object array, into the rest of
    the GameState / SharedIPC mapping, and feed the garbage ids and
    coordinates straight into the SmoothObj tracker. It is safe today
    only because the client clamps object_count to MAX_OBJECTS before
    writing the frame to the SHM (src/spacegl_client.c): a coupling the
    viewer cannot see, so a modified client — or any other producer of
    the same SHM layout — that removes or bypasses the clamp turns the
    loop into a silent OOB read of the shared buffer. The GPU-driven
    path was already correct: gdd_build_frame() clamps the same count
    to MAX_OBJECTS before walking the frame (src/spacegl_vulkan_gdd.c).
    Fix: the loop bound is now MAX_OBJECTS, the size of the array it
    indexes (the nextObjs[] / smoothObjs[] written in the same loop are
    MAX_NET_OBJECTS wide, so the tighter bound changes nothing for
    them); no behavior change for any legitimate frame, which always
    carries at most MAX_OBJECTS objects. Verified: clean rebuild of the
    spacegl_vulkan target (C23, -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic, no new
    warnings). Smoke test: pending (run by the user).

* Thu Oct 01 2026 nicolataibi <nicola.taibi.1967@gmail.com> - 2026.10.01.04
  - Client/Server radio: fixed the fleet (Level A, is_encrypted=0x01)
    messages lost on 15 of the 19 tunable ciphers (audit item B5:
    divergent cipher tables, the server knew only 4 of the 19). The
    client encrypted the rad messages with 19 ciphers (the full table
    in encrypt_payload() and in the PKT_MESSAGE receive path of
    src/spacegl_client.c), but the server fleet decryption in
    broadcast_message() (src/server/net.c) had a 4-slot table
    (AES-256-GCM, CHACHA20-POLY1305, ARIA-256-GCM, CAMELLIA-256-CTR)
    and resolved every other slot (enc seed, enc cast, enc rc4, ...)
    to AES-256-GCM no matter what the packet's crypto_algo said: the
    GCM tag check always failed, the message was dropped with
    "Frequency parity failure" and — the fleet path decrypts before
    relaying — it was not delivered to anyone, not even back to the
    sender. A fourth divergent copy of the same 4-slot table also
    lived in the server's own encrypt_payload() (adaptive relay
    re-encryption and send_server_msg): even a successfully decrypted
    fleet message was re-encrypted to a non-core recipient with
    AES-256-GCM while labeled with the recipient's slot: undecryptable
    on the client. Fix: the cipher table is now ONE shared
    client/server lookup — radio_cipher_for_algo() in
    include/radio_crypto.h — used by all four sites (client encrypt,
    client decrypt, server fleet decrypt, server encrypt), so the
    wire slot and the cipher agree on every endpoint by construction.
    Decision for the table contents: only the four modern ciphers are
    implemented (CRYPTO_AES -> AES-256-GCM, CRYPTO_CHACHA ->
    CHACHA20-POLY1305, CRYPTO_ARIA -> ARIA-256-GCM, CRYPTO_CAMELLIA
    -> CAMELLIA-256-CTR); every other slot (SEED, CAST5, IDEA, 3DES,
    BLOWFISH, RC4, DES, the post-quantum-named slots 12-19, SM4, GOST,
    SALSA, and any 0 / 22..255 wire value) is a fixed alias of
    AES-256-GCM, the strongest of the four — no legacy/weak cipher is
    ever used on the wire. The fixed mapping also removes the
    environment dependence of the old tables: in this OpenSSL (3.x)
    the legacy ciphers exist only in the "legacy" provider that the
    system configuration deliberately leaves unloaded (the EVP_*
    constructors still return non-NULL while EVP_EncryptInit fails at
    runtime, so the old "!cipher" fallback could not detect the gap),
    and the name-based lookups SM4-CBC / GOST-KUZNYECHIK are absent
    from the build — a slot->cipher mapping that depends on the local
    OpenSSL state is exactly the divergence that caused B5. Verified:
    clean rebuild of both targets (C23, -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic, no
    new warnings) and of the test unit; new unit test
    tests/crypto_table_test (ctest crypto_table) pins the contract:
    every slot 1..21 (plus 0/22/255) resolves to a cipher, the four
    modern slots keep their cipher and AEAD flag, every other slot is
    the AES-256-GCM alias, the client-encrypt -> server-decrypt
    round-trip (16-byte IV, 16-byte GCM tag when AEAD) succeeds on
    ALL slots while a wrong key never passes, and the four
    frequencies that already worked keep their exact pre-fix
    resolution. Smoke test: pending (run by the user).

