    up to 65535 while the ciphertext is written into
    msg->text[65536]). The API accepted any plaintext up to the 65535
    clamp and fed it straight into EVP_EncryptUpdate/Final with
    msg->text as the output; with the padding ciphers (the CBC slots
    SEED, CAST5, IDEA, 3DES, BLOWFISH, DES, SM4) the PKCS padding
    rounds the output up to a whole block — a full extra block when the
    plaintext is block-aligned — so a 65520-byte plaintext already
    yields 65536 bytes of ciphertext: it fills msg->text to the last
    byte (zero margin — any input beyond the 65535 clamp, e.g. a
    modified client, would give 65552 bytes of ciphertext and 16 bytes
    past the buffer) and sets msg->length = 65536, outside the
    [0, 65535] wire range both endpoints re-validate
    (packet_message_length_valid, the B1 check): the receiving
    endpoint aborts the connection, and in the strict
    peer/private/exclusive relay the oversized packet is forwarded
    as-is, taking down the target client. Not reachable from the UI
    (the radio line clamps to 4 KB), but the API allowed it: a modified
    client tuned to a CBC frequency sending a 65520..65535-byte message
    produced such a packet, and the server copy exposes the same
    unbounded write in the adaptive relay (re-encryption per
    recipient). Fix: right after cipher selection, if the chosen
    cipher pads (EVP_CIPHER_get_block_size > 1 — the CBC slots; the
    AEAD/CTR/stream modes keep ciphertext == plaintext length and allow
    the full 65535) and the exact padded ciphertext length
    ((len + block) / block * block, computed with the cipher's own
    block size so it is exact for the 8-byte blocks too) would exceed
    65535, the payload is rejected: the client copy returns 0 (the
    send path already treats that as "Encryption failed. Message NOT
    sent.") and the server copy falls back to cleartext exactly like
    its existing encryption-failure paths. For the 16-byte CBC slots
    the bound lands exactly on reject-if-len > 65535 - 16 (max 65519),
    the 8-byte blocks allow up to 65527. The server cipher table
    currently resolves everything to AEAD/CTR (output == input), so its
    guard keeps the API bound exact for the day a CBC slot is added
    there. Verified: clean rebuild of both targets (C23, -Wall -Wextra
    -Wpedantic, no new warnings). Smoke test: pending (run by the user).

* Thu Oct 01 2026 nicolataibi <nicola.taibi.1967@gmail.com> - 2026.10.01.02
  - Client security: fixed an out-of-bounds write into the static update
    state in the PKT_UPDATE_DELTA parser (src/spacegl_client.c, audit item
    B3: network counts used as read_all() lengths without clamp). The
    delta path fed the raw wire values straight into the two
    variable-length reads into the static PacketUpdate current_state
    (~173 KB): the UPD_OBJECTS block did
    read_all(sock, current_state.objects, oc * sizeof(NetObject)) with oc
    an unvalidated network int32_t, and the UPD_COMBAT block did
    read_all(sock, current_state.beams, bc * sizeof(NetBeam)) with bc
    equally unvalidated — while the full PKT_UPDATE path already clamps
    object_count before its read. current_state.objects is
    NetObject[MAX_NET_OBJECTS] (1024) and current_state.beams is
    NetBeam[MAX_NET_BEAMS] (64), so a sick or hostile server answering
    with oc = 2000 wrote ~165 KB past the end of the object array into
    the static state (and bc > 64 overflowed the beam array the same
    way). Fix: clamp oc to [0, MAX_NET_OBJECTS] and bc to
    [0, MAX_NET_BEAMS] right after the length read and before the
    read_all, with the same negative-value handling the full PKT_UPDATE
    path (and the LRS path) already uses; no behavior change for
    legitimate traffic (a well-behaved server never reports more than
    its own arrays). Verified: clean rebuild of the client target (C23,
    -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic, no new warnings). Smoke test: pending
    (run by the user).

* Thu Oct 01 2026 nicolataibi <nicola.taibi.1967@gmail.com> - 2026.10.01.01
  - Server security: fixed an out-of-bounds key-table read in the fleet
    (Level A) decryption path of broadcast_message() (src/server/net.c,
    audit item B2: crypto_algo as an index without range check).
    msg->crypto_algo is a network uint8_t (0..255) while
    players[].algo_keys has only MAX_CRYPTO_ALGOS + 1 (22) rows: the
    decryption key was taken as players[sender_idx].algo_keys[algo]
    with the raw wire value. The range guard already existed in
    ensure_player_algo_key() (src/spacegl_server.c: k < 1 || k >
    MAX_CRYPTO_ALGOS -> return), but its result was ignored by the
    caller — after the no-op return the unvalidated index was used a
    few lines below, so a client (even a buggy one) sending a fleet
    message (is_encrypted=0x01, no private/peer/exclusive bit) with
    crypto_algo = 22..255 made the server read past the per-player key
    table and hand that out-of-bounds pointer to EVP_DecryptInit_ex: a
    remotely range-controlled heap over-read. The other two key lookups
    of the same file were already correct (the adaptive relay and
    send_server_msg both check 1..MAX_CRYPTO_ALGOS before indexing).
    Fix: the same guard at the fleet site, with the same out-of-range
    fallback those paths use (session key) — a bogus algorithm now
    simply fails the GCM tag check and the message is dropped with the
    usual “Frequency parity failure” reply; no connection drop, no
    behavior change for legitimate traffic (algorithms 1..21).
    Verified: clean rebuild of the server target (C23, -Wall -Wextra
    -Wpedantic, no new warnings). Smoke test: pending (run by the user).

