* Tue Sep 29 2026 nicolataibi <nicola.taibi.1967@gmail.com> - 2026.09.29.03
  - Server security: PacketMessage.length is now validated on the server
  side (remote DoS fix). The PKT_MESSAGE parser (spacegl_server.c) only
  branched on "length > 0 && length < 65536" to decide whether to read
  the payload, but kept the attacker-controlled length in the packet
  otherwise: with length = -5 the value reached broadcast_message()
  (server/net.c) where it is converted to size_t — memcpy(..., c_len)
  with c_len = (size_t)-5, EVP_DecryptUpdate(..., msg->text, msg->length)
  on the fleet path and pkt_size = offsetof(...) + msg->length in the
  relay — a ~4 GB copy/read of heap garbage (crash, or garbage fanned
  out to every client). The client already validated exactly this
  ("Protocol error: message length out of range") while the server did
  not. Both endpoints now use one canonical check
  (packet_message_length_valid, include/network.h: length must be in
  [0, 65535], the 65536-byte text buffer minus the NUL) and the server
  drops the connection on violation — the payload cannot be skipped
  without desynchronizing the stream — reaping the player slot first so
  the closed fd cannot be recycled into the old identity (same pattern
  as the zztop/unsafe-name DoS hardening). New regression contract test
  tests/pkt_length_test.c pins the [0, 65535] bound against the wire
  format and proves the pre-fix sink arithmetic was out of bounds for
  the attack values.

